{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Monoculture in agriculture is often a result of top-down optimization, whereas in decentralized networks it emerges bottom-up.","A decentralized network might not have a global mechanism to enforce diversity, unlike a planned agricultural rotation system."],"overturn_conditions":["Empirical evidence shows decentralized operators naturally maintain diverse dependency stacks.","A large service is proven unable to enforce internal diversity due to organizational silos."],"shared_structure":["Independent agents optimizing for individual utility converge on identical strategies.","Convergence on identical strategies creates an emergent systemic vulnerability.","The vulnerability is hidden during normal operation and only manifests during a shared stress event."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a monoculture crop is uniformly susceptible to a specific pathogen, a decentralized network is uniformly susceptible to a specific dependency failure.","The appearance of independence in decentralized nodes is as illusory as the genetic diversity of a cloned crop."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small services will exhibit higher rates of correlated failure than a single large service with equivalent internal redundancy, because rational economic pressure drives independent operators toward identical 'best-in-class' dependencies, creating a hidden monoculture.","counterconditions":["The large service's internal redundancy relies on identical components, failing to achieve design independence.","Decentralized operators intentionally choose suboptimal or diverse dependencies to maintain true independence.","The shared 'best-in-class' dependency used by the decentralized network is formally verified to never fail.","The large service lacks the budget to scale formal verification of isolation linearly."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Independent operators face similar market incentives and risk assessments, leading them to uniformly adopt the same 'optimal' technologies (e.g., the same cloud provider, TLS library, or DNS root). This convergence creates an unmonitored shared dependency graph. While component-level failures appear uncorrelated, a failure in the shared 'best-in-class' dependency triggers a correlated failure across the decentralized network. A large service can use its concentrated resources to enforce internal diversity and formally verify isolation, avoiding the monoculture trap.","optional_refs":["b541b893af0360d00b8413afc003de605643ec970a3798265e7852b357fe0cd5","286e3232567e30e109fd5626102f029c2b31c89655104a1286510b12824e4103"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["b541b893af0360d00b8413afc003de605643ec970a3798265e7852b357fe0cd5","286e3232567e30e109fd5626102f029c2b31c89655104a1286510b12824e4103"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"simulation_proposals":[]}